course: dictionary
lesson: 3561
title: Ferns, the plants with feathery leaves
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fern is a green plant with no flowers. Its long, feathery leaves are called fronds. Ferns do not make seeds. They make new plants from tiny spores, which grow in brown dots under the fronds.
explain: Ferns are very old. They lived before the dinosaurs, and giant tree ferns long ago helped to make coal. Many ferns still like damp, shady places, such as woods and rainforests. «Borneo's forest trees are hung with giant ferns and other plants that grow on branches.»
explain: A young frond starts as a tight curl called a fiddlehead, and it slowly unrolls as it grows. In New Zealand the silver fern is a national symbol, so you will often see it on sports shirts. When you meet the word fern in a text, look for the clues: green, feathery leaves, damp woods, spores.
fact: A fern is a green plant with no flowers.
fact: Ferns have long, feathery leaves called fronds.
fact: Ferns make new plants from spores, not seeds.
fact: The spores grow in brown dots under the fronds.
fact: Some ferns grow high up on branches, catching rain and fallen bits.
exercise: «Ferns make new plants from tiny spores, not seeds.» What do ferns use to make new plants? | Flowers | Petals | Seeds | *Spores
exercise: Where do ferns like to grow? | Hot, dry deserts | On top of snowy mountains | *Damp, shady places like woods | Under the sea
exercise: What are the long, feathery leaves of a fern called? | Petals | *Fronds | Spores | Seeds
test: A young frond starts as a tight curl called a what? | Root | *Fiddlehead | Cone | Bud
test: «Borneo's forest trees are hung with giant ferns and other plants that grow on branches.» Where do these ferns grow? | Inside a rock | Under the sea | *Up on branches of trees | In the soil at the bottom of the forest
exam: Which sentence about ferns is true? | Ferns are animals that run fast | Ferns only grow in hot deserts | Ferns have bright flowers and make seeds | *Ferns have no flowers and make new plants from spores
